at with high  confidence  95 percent say which we the number that  we'll pick  um  that that range contains the true value  with prob with that confidence  in other words well  to me specific if you want a confidence  interval of 95 we take  the estimate of our slope plus or minus  twice the estimated standard error  and this  if errors are normally distributed which  we typically assume approximately this  will contain the true value the true  slope  with  probability of  0.95  okay okay so what we get from that is a  confidence interval which is a lower  point  and an upper point  which contains a true value with  probability 0.95 under repeated sampling  now what does that mean that's going to  be a little tricky to interpret that  let's see a little more detail what that  actually means  let's think of a true value of beta beta  one  which might be zero in particular which  means there's the slope is zero  and now let's draw a line at beta one  now imagine that we draw a data set like  the one we drew and we get a confidence  interval from this formula  and that confidence interval looks like  this  so i can this one contains a true value  because i've got the line that's in  between in the bracket  now i get a second data set in the same  population and i form its confidence  interval  from that data set  it looks a little different but it also  contains a true value  now i get a third data set  and i do the least squares computation  like form the confidence interval  uh unluckily it doesn't contain the true  value it's sitting over here it's  there's it's above beta one beta one's  below the whole interval  and i get another data set maybe i miss  on the other side this time  and then get another data set and i  contain the true value  so we can imagine doing this this  experiment many many times each time  getting a new data set from the  population doing the least squares  computation and forming the confidence  interval  and what the theory tells us is that  if i form say 100 confidence intervals  100 of these brackets  i will um  95 of the time they will contain the  true value  the other 5 of the time they'll not  contain the true value  so i can be i can be pretty sure that  the the the the interval contains the  true value  if i form the confidence interval in  this way i can be sure at probability  0.95  so for the advertising data the  confidence interval  uh for beta1  is  0.042.053  this is for i think it's for tv sales  so this tells me that  the true slope for for tv advertising  um  is  first of all it's it's greater than zero  in other words having tv advertising  does have a positive effect on  sales as one would expect  okay so that completes our discussion of  standard errors and confidence intervals  in the next segment we'll talk about  hypothesis testing which is a closely  related idea to confidence intervals

